I think I was able to come up with something that will work, after some back and forth between this approach and a couple of others which proved to be dead-ends. This fix is dependent on getting rid of the event logging ops, in any case the declarative schema changer event logging could do with some love. I have some ideas for that that I want to try out, I'll do so next week, and will then rebase the head commit 58d0c209778b393d6ff066b4eaf8c8caef789b6a which addresses #88294 on top of that that. I'll probably split it up to break off some small pieces that can live as stand-alone changes. Still, the hugeness of the diff on the data-driven test specs is unavoidable, even if I'm able to reduce some of the noise. In the meantime, I'd welcome eyeballs on this to provide a sanity check. 

Here's an example of what the new plans look like. Basically each statement phase only produces in-memory side effects which are undone at pre-commit time and then everything is re-planned. Fortunately, there are very few mutation ops which have side-effects which can't be undone, most are about jobs which we don't want at statement time anyway, excluding those there's RemoveDatabaseRoleSettings which deletes rows in that system table via the internal executor and RefreshStats which there's no point in running eagerly in-txn. Looking ahead, it appears that if we want role settings or users any other system table to be "undoable" in the same way that descriptor, zones and comments already are, we'll have to mediate their access somehow, which shouldn't be the end of the world to do (the users table is already cached, for instance). 

